'''Inflexión''' is a hand-built esoteric programming language whose surface syntax flows from the morphological features of Rioplatense Argentine Spanish. The six grammatical-semantic mappings — between '''number''', '''mood''', '''aspect''', the {{w|Spanish copula|''ser''/''estar'' split}}, {{w|Clitic doubling|clitic ordering}}, and {{w|Diminutive#Spanish|diminutive / augmentative morphology}} — carry the language's semantic load. A program in Inflexión that reads as Spanish prose to a Spanish-speaking reader is, simultaneously, a program in which number, mood, aspect, and copular choice determine what the program does.

Inflexión joins a small lineage of inflection-driven, non-English natural-language esolangs: [[Lingua::Romana::Perligata|''Lingua::Romana::Perligata'']] (Latin, Damian Conway, 2000), Espro (Esperanto, 2015, idea-only), [[Tampio]] (Finnish, Iikka Hauhio, ~2017–present), and [[Wenyan]] (Classical Chinese, Lingdong Huang, 2019). It is the first member of that lineage to use a living [[:Category:Romance|Romance]] language, and the first (to the best of the author's knowledge) to make the ''ser''/''estar'', mood, aspect, clitic-ordering, diminutive, and number-agreement systems jointly load-bearing.

== Language overview ==

Inflexión's six grammatical-semantic mappings are:

* '''Number''' (singular vs plural). The article on a noun marks the cell's shape: ''el precio'' is a scalar binding, ''los precios'' is a collection. Arithmetic on a collection broadcasts elementwise. Article–noun number mismatch is a runtime error.
* '''Mood''' (indicative / subjunctive / imperative). Indicative ''es'' / ''está'' performs eager binding; subjunctive ''esté'' marks deferred / hypothetical clauses (used by both ''Cuando ... esté en ...'' deferred bindings and ''Mientras ... esté en ...'' loop conditions); imperative ''Decí'', ''Hablá'', ''Hacé'' perform side effects.
* '''Aspect''' (perfective vs imperfective). The verb suffix selects evaluation strategy: ''sumó los precios'' is eager (returns a value); ''sumaba los precios'' is lazy (returns a stream of partial results). The eight-letter shared stem and the two-letter aspect-marker carry the entire eager-versus-lazy distinction.
* '''The ser/estar split.''' ''El x es V'' creates an immutable binding; ''El x está en V'' creates a mutable cell. Mutation goes through ''Hacé que el x esté en V''; an attempt to re-bind a ''ser'' cell is a runtime error.
* '''Clitic ordering.''' The fixed Spanish enclitic sequence ''se-te-me-lo-le'' on a ''vos''-imperative verb (''Transferíselo'', ''Dámelo'') routes arguments by position in that order.
* '''Diminutive and augmentative morphology.''' The diminutive suffixes ''-ito''/''-ita''/''-cito''/''-cita'' scale numeric values by ½; augmentative suffixes ''-ón''/''-ona''/''-azo''/''-aza'' scale by 2 or 4. A diminutive on a function name (''busquito'' vs ''buscar'') invokes a cheap variant of the function.

The dialect is Rioplatense (Argentine), with ''vos''-form verbs throughout (''decí'', ''hacé'', ''hablá'', ''escuchá'') and lunfardo-aware vocabulary. The dialect choice is named and defended in the design paper.

The runtime adds a small set of operational primitives that do not themselves carry semantic load through morphology — they exist for the same reason ordinary programming languages have them. These include conditional dispatch via ''Si ... sino ...'', function definition via the relative-clause form ''La función f, que toma una x y una y, es ...'', recursion via parenthesised arguments ''fact (la n menos 1)'', string and list operations (length, character access, indexed read/write, concatenation, slicing), the arithmetic operators ''más'' / ''menos'' / ''por'' / ''entre'' / ''módulo'' (+, −, ×, /, mod), and stdin via ''Escuchá''.

== Examples ==

=== Hello, World! ===

<pre>
El saludo es "Hola, mundo".
Decilo.
</pre>

''Decilo'' is the ''vos''-imperative of ''decir'' (to say) with the third-person direct-object clitic ''lo'' enclitic, dereferencing the most-recent binding.

=== Cat ===

<pre>
Escuchá una línea en la entrada.
Decí la entrada.
</pre>

Reads one line from stdin and writes it to stdout. The article ''la'' is feminine because the binding name ''entrada'' (entry) is a feminine noun in Spanish.

=== Truth machine ===

<pre>
Escuchá un número en la entrada.
Si la entrada es 0, decí 0.
Mientras la entrada no esté en 0, decí 1.
</pre>

If the input is 0, prints ''0'' and halts. If the input is non-zero, prints ''1'' until the runtime's 100,000-iteration ''mientras'' safety cap fires and raises a runtime error. The classical truth machine loops forever; Inflexión's runtime declines to honour that, on the ground that unbounded iteration is rarely what a Spanish-prose program is asking for.

=== FizzBuzz ===

<pre>
La i está en 1.
Mientras la i no esté en 101,
  si la i es divisible por 15, decí "FizzBuzz";
  sino, si la i es divisible por 3, decí "Fizz";
  sino, si la i es divisible por 5, decí "Buzz";
  sino, decí la i;
  y que la i esté en la i más 1.
</pre>

Note the article ''la'' on the single-letter variable ''i'' — letters of the Spanish alphabet are feminine (''la letra i''), so single-letter variable names take ''la'' rather than ''el''.

=== Fibonacci (recursive) ===

<pre>
La función fib, que toma una n, es
  si la n es 0, entonces 0;
  sino, si la n es 1, entonces 1;
  sino, fib (la n menos 1) más fib (la n menos 2).
Decí fib 10.
</pre>

Demonstrates recursion via the parenthesised-argument form. The parentheses around ''la n menos 1'' disambiguate where the recursive call's argument ends — without them, the parser would read ''(fib la n) menos 1''.

=== Quicksort ===

<pre>
La función pequeños, que toma una xs, un pivote, una idx y una acc, es
  si la idx es mayor que el largo de la xs, entonces la acc;
  sino, si el idx-ésimo de la xs es menor que el pivote,
    entonces pequeños la xs el pivote (la idx más 1) (unir la acc y [el idx-ésimo de la xs]);
    sino, pequeños la xs el pivote (la idx más 1) la acc.
La función grandes, que toma una xs, un pivote, una idx y una acc, es
  si la idx es mayor que el largo de la xs, entonces la acc;
  sino, si el idx-ésimo de la xs no es menor que el pivote,
    entonces grandes la xs el pivote (la idx más 1) (unir la acc y [el idx-ésimo de la xs]);
    sino, grandes la xs el pivote (la idx más 1) la acc.
La función quicksort, que toma una xs, es
  si el largo de la xs es menor que 2, entonces la xs;
  sino, unir (quicksort (pequeños la xs (el primero de la xs) 2 []))
         y (unir [el primero de la xs] y (quicksort (grandes la xs (el primero de la xs) 2 []))).
La lista está en [5, 2, 8, 1, 9, 3, 7, 4, 6].
Decí quicksort la lista.
</pre>

Pure-functional 3-way quicksort using list concatenation (''unir A y B''), dynamic list literals (''[el idx-ésimo de la xs]''), and the negated-comparison condition ''no es menor que'' (≥). No mutation; recursive descent only.

=== Brainfuck interpreter ===

The example corpus includes a working [[Brainfuck]] interpreter written in Inflexión, ~3000 characters, that runs the canonical 106-character ''Hello, World!'' BF program end-to-end. This serves as the Turing-completeness witness for the language. See the source at <code>examples/brainfuck.infl</code> in the implementation repository.

== Computational class ==

Turing-complete. The witness is the Brainfuck interpreter described above. The design paper argues that ''mientras'' iteration combined with self-referential ''ser'' recursion is in principle sufficient for Turing-completeness; the BF interpreter establishes it concretely.

A practical caveat: the Python implementation imposes a 100,000-iteration safety cap on ''mientras'' loops. Programs that intentionally iterate without bound (such as a textbook truth machine) terminate with a runtime error when the cap fires. The cap can be configured at runtime if desired; the default reflects a design choice rather than a theoretical limit.

== Implementation ==

The reference implementation is a Python interpreter at [https://github.com/Roderick-Consulting-Inc/inflexion github.com/Roderick-Consulting-Inc/inflexion]. The lexer combines [https://spacy.io/ spaCy]'s ''es_core_news_sm'' Spanish morphological model with a custom rule layer for Rioplatense ''vos''-imperatives and enclitic clitic stacks (''Transferíselo'', ''Dámelo''), the ordinal-positional suffix ''-ésimo'' for variable indexing into lists (''el i-ésimo de la lista''), and the diminutive/augmentative suffix table. The parser is hand-written recursive-descent; the interpreter is big-step recursive over the AST.

A live web playground for the runtime is at [https://babel.roderickc.com babel.roderickc.com], with all twenty example programs runnable from the "Inflexión" tab.

